#[doc("""
Surface helpers on top of the `Process` effect. Module path:
`import process`; qualified calls land as `process.start(...)`.

Two ways to launch a child. `start` lets it inherit the parent's
standard fds, so it shares the terminal. `start_piped` instead
attaches pipes to its stdin and stdout, which is what "page this
output" and "shell out and capture" need; `write_stdin`,
`read_stdout` and `close_stdin` move the bytes.

POSIX only.
""")]

#[doc("""
Start a child process via `fork(2)` + `execvp(3)`. Returns an
opaque `Child` carrying the OS pid; the child inherits the
parent's standard fds, so it shares the terminal. Use
`start_piped` to capture or feed them instead. Panics if the fork
or exec fails.
""")]
pub fn start(cmd: String, args: [String]) : Child / Process =
  Process.start(cmd, args)

#[doc("""
Start a child process with pipes on its stdin and stdout instead
of the parent's fds; stderr still inherits, so the child's
diagnostics stay visible. Returns `Err(strerror)` if the pipe or
fork fails.

Feed it with `write_stdin`, drain it with `read_stdout`, signal
end-of-input with `close_stdin`, then reap it with `wait`.

A pipe holds a bounded amount of data — about 64 KB on Linux, 16 KB
on macOS — and a child blocks once it fills. Drain `read_stdout`
while the child runs rather than waiting first, or a child that
outgrows the buffer deadlocks against a parent that never reads.
""")]
pub fn start_piped(cmd: String, args: [String]) : Result[Child, String] / Process =
  Process.start_piped(cmd, args)

#[doc("""
Write a string to a piped child's stdin and return how many bytes
went through. Returns `Err(strerror)` on failure — notably
`Err("Broken pipe")` when the child already closed its read end
(a pager the user quit), which is an error here rather than a
fatal `SIGPIPE`.

Blocks the OS thread, not the fiber, and blocks while the child is
not draining a full pipe.
""")]
pub fn write_stdin(c: Child, s: String) : Result[Int, String] / Process =
  Process.write_stdin(c, s)

#[doc("""
Read at most `n` bytes from a piped child's stdout. An empty
string means end of output: the child closed its stdout, normally
by exiting. Returns `Err(strerror)` on a read failure.

This reads what is available up to `n` rather than filling the
buffer, so loop until it returns `""` to consume everything.

Blocks the OS thread, not the fiber, until at least one byte or
EOF is available.
""")]
pub fn read_stdout(c: Child, n: Int) : Result[String, String] / Process =
  Process.read_stdout(c, n)

#[doc("""
Close a piped child's stdin so it sees end-of-input. A child that
reads until EOF — a pager, `sort`, `wc` — only finishes once this
happens, so call it before `wait`. Idempotent, and `Ok` for a
child started without pipes.
""")]
pub fn close_stdin(c: Child) : Result[Unit, String] / Process =
  Process.close_stdin(c)

#[doc("""
Read a piped child's stdout to end of output and return it whole.
Loops `read_stdout` in 64 KB chunks, so the child never blocks on
a full pipe regardless of how much it emits.

Returns `Err(strerror)` if any underlying read fails. Does not
reap the child — call `wait` afterwards for its `Exit`.
""")]
pub fn read_all_stdout(c: Child) : Result[String, String] / Process =
  read_all_loop(c, "")

fn read_all_loop(c: Child, acc: String) : Result[String, String] / Process =
  match Process.read_stdout(c, 65536) {
    Err(msg) -> Err(msg)
    Ok(chunk) ->
      if string_length(chunk) == 0 { Ok(acc) }
      else { read_all_loop(c, string_concat(acc, chunk)) }
  }

#[doc("""
Wait for a child process to terminate and return its exit status
as an `Exit` variant (`Exited(code)` or `Signaled(signo)`).
Returns `Err(strerror)` if `waitpid` itself fails (e.g. ECHILD if
the child was already reaped). Blocks the OS thread, not the fiber.
""")]
pub fn wait(c: Child) : Result[Exit, String] / Process =
  Process.wait(c)

#[doc("""
Send a POSIX signal to a child process via `kill(pid, sig)`,
lifting the libc errno to `Err(strerror)` on failure (e.g. ESRCH
if the child was already reaped, EPERM on permission denial).

`sig` is the raw POSIX signo (e.g. 9 for SIGKILL, 15 for SIGTERM,
2 for SIGINT); see `signal(7)` for the full list.
""")]
pub fn kill(c: Child, sig: Int) : Result[Unit, String] / Process =
  Process.kill(c, sig)

#[doc("""
Terminate the current process with the given exit code via libc
`_exit(code)` (NOT `exit(3)`), so stdio buffers are NOT flushed;
`print` everything you want printed before calling this.

The op never resumes (return type `Nothing`) and Cancel handlers
do NOT run — this is a hard kill of the current process.
""")]
pub fn exit(code: Int) : Nothing / Process =
  Process.exit(code)
